{"ts": 1789784522.9904609, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "Consistent. Every noise-feature third leg lands in 1.08\u20131.24, comfortably below {2,26,31}'s 1.391. I'll go with {2, 26, 31}, all long.\n\nNow the harder question: what OOS Sharpe do I actually predict? The in-sample 1.391 is an upward-biased estimate, and I should not submit it as my belief.\n\n**Sources of shrinkage:**\n\n1. **Selection bias.** I chose this spec as the best of ~45 evaluated combinations, and the three features as the top of 40. Winner's curse is real. But it's *much smaller here than in a typical search*, because features 2, 26, and 31 weren't marginal picks \u2014 they were separated from the noise floor by a wide gap (0.97/0.77/0.67 vs. a noise band topping out near 0.41). I'm not selecting noise that happened to look good; I'm selecting features whose signal exceeds plausible sampling error by several standard errors. The noise-feature control tests confirm this directly.\n\n2. **Sampling error.** With n=5000 (~20 years daily), the standard error on an annualized Sharpe of ~1.4 is roughly \u221a((1+S\u00b2/2)/T) annualized \u2248 0.24. So even absent selection, \u00b10.25 of wobble.\n\n3. **Regime decay.** This is the largest and least measurable term. In-sample and out-of-sample differ not just by sampling noise but by the world changing \u2014 feature relationships decay, get arbitraged, or shift with regime. I cannot measure this from in-sample data at all, and it is essentially always negative in expectation.\n\nNetting these: I shrink the 1.391 substantially. Selection bias costs maybe 0.1 (small, given the clean signal separation); decay and regime drift cost considerably more, perhaps 0.3\u20130.4. That centers me near **0.95**.\n\nFor the standard deviation, the temptation is to quote the ~0.24 sampling error. That would be a mistake \u2014 it would treat the decay term as known when it's the dominant uncertainty. I set **sd = 0.40**, wide enough that the interval spans genuinely different worlds: near-zero if the relationship has substantially decayed, ~1.4 if it holds up as well in-sample performance suggests."}
