{"ts": 1789784565.041327, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Submitted\n\n**Specification:** features 2, 26, 31 \u2014 all long, equal weight\n**In-sample Sharpe:** 1.391\n**Predicted OOS Sharpe:** 0.95 \u00b1 0.40\n\n### How I got there\n\nI evaluated all 40 features individually first. Four separated clearly from the pack \u2014 2 (0.97), 26 (0.77), 31 (0.67), 28 (0.41) \u2014 while the other 36 sat in a \u00b10.2\u20130.4 band. That band is roughly what pure noise produces at this sample size: a true-zero feature has a standard error near 0.22 on annualized Sharpe, so the best of 36 null features landing around 0.4 is unremarkable. The gap between the top three and that floor is what gave me confidence these are real.\n\nI then swept the pairs and triples of the four candidates. {2, 26, 31} won at 1.391, and the combination beats its parts, so the features carry somewhat independent information.\n\n### The checks that mattered most\n\n**Sign verification.** Flipping any single sign collapsed performance (1.391 \u2192 0.63, 0.50, 0.27). Had the flips been close, it would suggest I was fitting signs to noise. The asymmetry says these are genuine directional effects.\n\n**Noise-feature controls.** This was the key test. I took the strong pair {2, 26} (1.236) and added the best-looking *noise* features as a third leg. Every one landed at 1.08\u20131.24 \u2014 at or **below** the pair alone. So spurious features don't manufacture improvement here; dilution slightly outweighs any overfit gain. Feature 31's lift to 1.391 is therefore real signal rather than a selection artifact, which materially reduced how much I shrank my forecast.\n\n### Why I predict well below 1.391\n\nReporting the in-sample number as a belief would be the central error. Three things separate it from expected OOS performance:\n\n- **Selection bias** \u2014 best of ~45 evaluated specs. Present, but modest here given the wide signal/noise separation and the control results.\n- **Sampling error** \u2014 roughly \u00b10.24 at n=5000, irreducible.\n- **Regime decay** \u2014 the biggest term, and invisible from in-sample data. Relationships weaken, get arbitraged away, or shift. Always negative in expectation.\n\nThat last term drove the wide **sd of 0.40**. Quoting the ~0.24 sampling error would have implied I could measure the decay, which I can't. The interval I gave spans meaningfully different futures: close to zero if the edge has substantially eroded, near 1.4 if it holds. My point estimate sits closer to the optimistic end than pure skepticism would suggest, because the control tests gave concrete evidence the signal isn't an artifact of my search."}
